RESOLV.CONF(5)              File Formats              RESOLV.CONF(5)

NAME
    resolv.conf - configuration of the name resolver

SYNOPSIS
    /etc/resolv.conf

DESCRIPTION
    getaddrinfo(3) reads this file for every name that is neither
    numeric, localhost nor listed in /etc/hosts. dhcpc(1) writes it from
    a lease and net(1) apply writes it from /etc/network. Each line holds
    a keyword and its values:

        nameserver ADDR       names a DNS server; three are used at most
        search DOMAIN...      sets the search list of at most six domains
        domain DOMAIN         sets a search list of one domain
        options ndots:N       sets how many dots make a name be tried as
                              given first (default 1, at most 15)

    The last search or domain line wins. A name that ends with a dot is
    tried alone. A name with at least ndots dots is tried as given and
    then with each search domain; a name with fewer dots is tried with
    each search domain first and as given last. Only a negative answer
    moves on to the next candidate.

    Every process keeps the answers it received for the smallest TTL of
    the records used, at most one hour, in a cache of 32 names. A
    negative answer is kept for the TTL of the SOA record that came with
    it or its MINIMUM field, whichever is smaller (RFC 2308), again at
    most one hour; a negative answer without an SOA record, a server
    failure and an answer with TTL 0 are not kept.

ENVIRONMENT
    RESOLV_CONF names another file, which tests use.

SEE ALSO
    dhcpc(1), net(1), index(7)
